course: dictionary
lesson: 4753
title: Hotel in context
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A hotel is a building where people pay to sleep and eat when they are travelling or on holiday. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it. «Picture a wedding in a hotel near Galway» has guests, a bridesmaid and a best man, so a hotel can be a place for a party too.
explain: Clues help you guess. In «Turtle hatchlings climb out of their nests at night and head for the brightest horizon», the words nests, night and sea tell you what is going on. Then look at «street and hotel lights can lead them the wrong way». Hotel lights are bright lights in a place where people stay, so they can confuse baby turtles.
explain: Check your guess by swapping in your meaning. Does «a building where people pay to sleep» make sense in «His foster carer says, "We're going to Cornwall, you're coming." "The hotel pool's peng," he tells his mates»? Yes, so you know the hotel is somewhere they stay on a trip. Peng is slang for great, so the pool must be great.
fact: A hotel is a building where people pay to stay the night.
fact: Hotels often have a reception desk, rooms with keys or key cards, and often a restaurant or pool.
fact: You check in when you arrive at a hotel and check out when you leave.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, «hotel» can also mean a pub.
fact: For safety, learn where the fire exits are in any hotel, and stay with your adults.
exercise: What is a hotel? | A shop that sells food and drinks | A park with a playground | *A building where people pay to sleep and eat when travelling | A school for young swimmers
exercise: Mum says, «We check out at ten tomorrow.» What does check out mean? | Arrive and unpack your bags | Go for a swim in the pool | Ask for a bigger room | *Leave the hotel and hand back your room
exercise: Which of these would you usually find at a hotel reception desk? | *A check-in desk where you give your name | A room key or key card | A tractor | A football
test: «Picture a wedding in a hotel near Galway.» Where is the wedding? | *In a hotel near Galway | On a train to Dublin | In a zoo | In a classroom
test: Why can hotel lights be a problem for turtle hatchlings? | They help the hatchlings find food | They stop the hatchlings climbing out of the nest | They make the sea too warm | *They can lead the hatchlings the wrong way, away from the sea
exam: You arrive at a hotel with your family. What should you learn first for safety? | The price of every room | The name of the chef | *Where the fire exits are | How to swim underwater
